Given the areas of three faces of a right rectangular prism are $24 \hspace{.6mm} \mathrm{cm}^2$, $32 \hspace{.6mm} \mathrm{cm}^2$, and $48 \hspace{.6mm} \mathrm{cm}^2$, we aim to find the volume of the prism. 

Let the dimensions of the prism be $x$, $y$, and $z$. The areas of the three faces are given by:
\begin{align*}
xy &= 24, \\
yz &= 32, \\
xz &= 48.
\end{align*}

To find the volume, we need to determine $xyz$. We can do this by multiplying all three equations together:
\begin{align*}
(xy)(yz)(xz) &= 24 \cdot 32 \cdot 48 \\
(xyz)^2 &= 24 \cdot 32 \cdot 48.
\end{align*}

Taking the square root of both sides gives us:
\begin{align*}
xyz &= \sqrt{24 \cdot 32 \cdot 48}.
\end{align*}

Simplifying the square root of the product:
\begin{align*}
xyz &= \sqrt{24} \cdot \sqrt{32} \cdot \sqrt{48} \\
xyz &= \sqrt{2^3} \cdot \sqrt{2^5} \cdot \sqrt{2^4 \cdot 3} \\
xyz &= 2^{3/2} \cdot 2^{5/2} \cdot 2^2 \cdot \sqrt{3} \\
xyz &= 2^{3/2 + 5/2 + 2} \cdot \sqrt{3} \\
xyz &= 2^6 \cdot \sqrt{3} \\
xyz &= 64 \cdot \sqrt{3}.
\end{align*}

Therefore, the volume of the prism is $\boxed{64 \hspace{.6mm} \mathrm{cm}^3}$.

